A||||7||FIEROCIOUS||Exhausting Idea?||||5||
Z||000000||FIEROCIOUS||02-14-2000||01:19 AM||||Does anyone know if a cast iron exhaust manifold from a early cavalier or the like would fit the Fiero?<BR>I have the "How to rebuild your GM V-6 60 engine" and saw a picture of an exhaust manifold on the front of the project car, it apears to be able to fit the fiero.<BR>This exhaust manifold doesn't look like much of a performance loss, and looks actualy to gain some flow!<P>Has anyone thought of this? It would solve the cracked front manifold problem!<P> ||||reg||5
Z||000001||mwbackus||02-14-2000||02:47 AM||||A similar thought occured to me the other day when I was at the local yard.  It seemed that this manifold design would yield itself well to a turbo application, since both mainfolds connect uniformly to a short crossover with the downpipe in the middle(like a T), exiting directly over the transmission.  One would have to measure carefully to determine if this was feasable.<P>Mike||||reg||1
Z||000002||Standard||02-14-2000||02:47 AM||||The exhaust manifolds would probably fit, the problem would be the crossover pipe.  Probably wouldn't be able to use it with a stock Fiero exhaust, but it would be a cheap alternative if it fit.  <P>Wow.. same time. spooky.  my name is Mike, too. coincidence?  i think not...  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif"><p>[This message has been edited by Standard (edited 02-14-2000).]||||reg||1
Z||000003||Oreif||02-14-2000||11:52 AM||||I know a few people that have tried to use exhaust manifolds from other cars (cavaliers, Grand Prixs, etc) You cannot use the Fiero Y pipe completely. The only way is to have a good exhaust place "build" one using your exsisting Y pipe. The cost is about $200. I was going to do it but opted for the Sprint Headers. It costs more but looks and performs very well. I installed them myself so the only labor was my time.<BR>  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/header.jpg">  <p>[This message has been edited by Oreif (edited 02-14-2000).]||||reg||1
Z||000004||mwbackus||02-14-2000||01:00 PM||||To better illustrate the concept I was referring to above, please compare the crossover pipe used by Dennis Lagrua on his DIY turbo system to that of the exhaust in question.  <P> <IMG SRC="http://members.xoom.com/_XMCM/dlagrua/Turbo15.jpg"> <P><BR>The only appreciable differences are; the exit (turbo attachment point in the above image) is directed downwards rather than to the rear, and it runs both higher and closer to the intake manifold.  Perhaps an elbow with a turbo flange on one end would be all that is needed to mount a turbo on this crossover design.  I had considered a turbo system such as this in the past, but I found the cost of having a custom crossover made in this local far exceed what I had anticipated.  This may be a viable alternative.<P>Mike||||reg||1
Z||000005||FIEROCIOUS||02-14-2000||01:40 PM||||Funny thing, my name is Mike too! I am doing a turbo upgrade got the turbo yesterday! T3 from a ford thats how I saw the manifold and figured it would work! <BR>Now which cars are the best canidates?<BR>What do you get the front manifold off of?  What do you get the rear manifold off of?<BR>This as martha stewart says <BR>"Is a good thing!"<P>Mike<BR>            ||||reg||10
Z||000006||87PegasusGT||02-14-2000||03:21 PM||||Fierocious: Please keep me updated on your progress, because I am also doing the T3 on my built 3.4. Any info would be appreciated.||||reg||1
Z||000007||FIEROCIOUS||02-15-2000||06:22 AM||||Turbos are the thing in Utah?<BR>I'm helping Kidzwheelz set the world on fire <BR>"His words!" with his turbo duke at the moment.<BR>I won't start on mine till end of this years race season.<BR>As it looks so far the cast manifold is definatly the way to go!<BR>So has anyone used a cast manifold on their Fiero?<BR>Every time I ask a question it's always I had a cousins friend sisters uncle that might of done (blank). <BR>Has anyone actually personaly done or seen first hand a cast exhaust manifold on a Fiero?<BR>Thanks everyone for their help!||||reg||1
